We propose a program toward a holographic representation theory of the non-scalar bosonic discrete series $\Pi^\pm_{p,q}$, $p,q\in\mathbb{N}$, $1\leqslant q\leqslant p$, of the de Sitter ($\mathrm{dS}_{4}$) group $G=\mathrm{SO}_0(1,4)$. The proposal exploits two complementary realizations of the same unitary irreducible representation (UIR): a bounded-domain realization on an open ball $\mathbb{B}$ modeling the hyperbolic space $G/\mathrm{SO}(4)$, with $\partial\mathbb{B}\simeq\mathbb{S}^{3}$, and a covariant spacetime realization on $\mathrm{dS}_{4}$, with conformal boundaries $\mathscr{I}^{\pm}\simeq\mathbb{S}^{3}$, in which the physical UIR is obtained through the corresponding gauge reduction. This leads to two boundary constructions and an explicit compatibility problem,
  \begin{align*}
  \mathbb{B}
  \;\overset{?\mathrm{hol}}{\longleftrightarrow}\;
  \partial\mathbb{B}\simeq\mathbb{S}^{3}
  \quad\overset{?}{\simeq}\quad
  \mathscr{I}^{\pm}\simeq\mathbb{S}^{3}
  \;\overset{?\mathrm{hol}}{\longleftrightarrow}\;
  \mathrm{dS}_{4} \,.
  \end{align*}
  The two outer problems seek explicit intertwiners between the bulk and boundary realizations, with their invariant norms, constraints, and physical quotients properly identified. The middle problem asks how to construct and normalize a unitary intertwiner between the two boundary realizations once both have been shown to carry the same UIR. The bounded picture exposes the unitary and harmonic structure of the representation, while the spacetime picture makes its covariant and gauge-theoretic structure manifest, allowing the two constructions to constrain and illuminate one another. The established scalar family $\Pi_{p,0}$, $p\in\mathbb{N}$, with its spherical realization on $\mathbb{S}^{3}$, provides the guiding prototype. Particular attention is given to ...

Let $RD_{\ell,t}^k(n)$ enumerates the number of $k$-colored simultaneously $l$-regular and $t$-distinct partitions. In this article, we establish some new infinite families of congruences modulo prime $p$, $p\geq5$ and modulo powers of 5 for $RD_{\ell,t}^k(n)$. For instance, for each $n\geq0$, $0\leq\alpha\leq{2k-2}$ and $k\geq1$,
  \begin{align*}
  \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}RD_{(5^{2k-1},5^{2k-1})}^2\big(5^{2k+\alpha-1}n+\frac{9\cdot5^{2k+\alpha-1}-2\cdot5^{2k-1}+1}{12}\big)q^n\equiv& 0\pmod{5^{k+\left[\frac{\alpha}{2}\right]}}
  \end{align*}

We count ordered commuting pairs in a full lattice \(\Lambda\) in a reductive Lie algebra \(\g\) over \(\Q\). For a fixed convex body \(B\) containing the origin in its interior, the count in \(HB\) is \(C_{\Lambda,B}H^{\dim\g+\dim Z(\g)}\) with a power-saving error. The leading term comes from pairs whose projections to each simple ideal are linearly dependent over its centroid. Their slopes index an absolutely convergent sum of section volumes for \(C_{\Lambda,B}\). Subtracting the contribution of dependent pairs from the finite-field Fourier sums gives cancellation at generic dependent frequencies. For independent frequencies, we bound the dimension of the locus where the corresponding hyperplane contains an irreducible component of a nonzero bracket fibre. For \(n\ge3\), the number of ordered commuting pairs in \(M_n(\Z)\) with entries of absolute value at most \(H\) is \[
  \frac{2(n+3)\zeta(n^2-2)}{(n+1)\zeta(n^2-1)}
  (2\lfloor H\rfloor+1)^{n^2+1}
  +O_n\!\left(H^{n^2+1-1/(n+1)}\right), \] confirming the exponent \(n^2+1\) predicted by Browning, Sawin and Wang~\cite{BSW}.

We study nonparametric regression over star-shaped function classes under strong adversarial contamination. The clean (original) observations $(\tilde{X} _{i},\tilde{Y} _{i})_{i=1} ^{n}$ are i.i.d. and satisfy $\tilde{Y} _{i}=f ^{*}(\tilde{X} _{i})+\xi _{i}$, where $f ^{*}\in \mathcal{F}$, $\tilde{X}$ is distributed as $\mathbb{P} _{X}$, and the noise is conditionally centered. An adversary may inspect the clean sample and replace at most $\lfloor \epsilon n\rfloor$ observation pairs. We assume that $\mathcal{F}\subset L _{2}(\mathbb{P} _{X})$ is star-shaped with finite diameter $d$.
  Under a Bernstein-type moment condition on function increments and a bounded conditional second moment for the noise, we establish the matching lower and upper bounds of order $\left((\eta ^{*}) ^{2} \vee \epsilon \right)\wedge d ^{2}$, uniformly over admissible star-shaped function classes, therefore characterizing the minimax rate of estimation error, where \begin{equation*}
  \eta ^{*}:=\sup\left\{\eta >0: n\eta^2\leq\log \mathcal M _{\mathcal{F}}^{\text{loc}}(\eta,c)\right\}. \end{equation*} Here $\log \mathcal{M} _{\mathcal{F}} ^{\text{loc}}(\eta,c)$ denotes the local metric entropy of $\mathcal{F}$ in $L _2(\mathbb{P} _{X})$, and $c$ is a sufficiently large constant. Under a uniform relative sub-Gaussian condition on function increments and noise, the lower bound is adjusted to $\left((\eta ^{*})^{2}\vee \epsilon ^{2}\right)\wedge d ^{2}$ while the upper bound improves to $\left((\eta ^{*})^{2}\vee \epsilon ^{2}\log ^{2}\left(\frac{e}{\epsilon }\right)\right)\wedge d ^{2}$, yielding minimax optimality up to logarithmic factors.

We study normalized solutions to the Born--Infeld problem
  \begin{equation*}
  -{\rm div}\left(\frac{\nabla u}{\sqrt{1-|\nabla u|^2}}\right)+\mu u=f(u)\ \hbox{ in }\mathbb{R}^N,\qquad \int_{\mathbb{R}^N}|u|^2\,dx=2m^2,
  \end{equation*}
  $N\ge3$, where $m>0$ is prescribed and $\mu>0$ is an unknown Lagrange multiplier. The nonlinearity $f$ is assumed to be superlinear at infinity, with no upper restriction on its growth, and to behave like $|s|^{p-2}s$ near zero, with $2<p<2^*$. Following a Lagrangian approach, we relate the action ground state level $a(\mu)$ of the prescribed frequency problem to the masses of its ground states by means of one-sided derivative formulas, which lead to the existence of normalized solutions. A distinctive feature of the Born--Infeld operator is that the gradient constraint makes the energy bounded from below on every mass sphere, even in the $L^2$-supercritical case. For $2+4/N\le p<2^*$ we obtain a threshold $c_*>0$ such that normalized action ground states exist for $m^2>c_*$, and none exist for $m^2<c_*$. In the $L^2$-subcritical case they exist for every $m^2>0$. We prove that every action ground state has constant sign and is radial up to a translation.

We study the two-dimensional two-component Coulomb gas in the canonical ensemble and at inverse temperature $\beta>2$. In this regime, the partition function diverges and the interaction needs to be cut off at a length scale $\lambda\in (0,1)$. Particles of opposite charges tend to pair into dipoles of length scale comparable to $\lambda$, which themselves can aggregate into multipoles. Despite the slow decay of dipole--dipole interactions, we construct a convergent cluster expansion around a hierarchical reference model that retains only intra-multipole interactions. This yields a large-deviation result for the number of $2p$-poles as well as a \textcolor{black}{free-energy expansion} as $N\to\infty$ and $\lambda\to0$, with three contributions: (i) the free energy of $N$ independent dipoles, (ii) a perturbative correction, and (iii) the contribution of a non-dilute subsystem.
  The perturbative term has two equivalent characterizations: (a) a convergent Mayer series built from non-divergent elementary dipole activities; and (b) a variational formula as the minimum of a large-deviation rate function for the empirical counts of $2p$-poles. The Mayer coefficients \textcolor{black}{should undergo} transitions at $\beta_p=4-\tfrac{2}{p}$\textcolor{black}{, and these thresholds} accumulate at $\beta=4$, which corresponds to the \textcolor{black}{Berezinskii--Kosterlitz--Thouless} transition in the low-dipole-density limit. \textcolor{black}{We prove that the limiting $p$-dipole activities are finite for $\beta>\beta_p$.}
  The non-dilute system corresponds to the contribution of large dipoles. For $2<\beta<4$, the independent-dipole dilution estimate predicts the critical length scale $R_{\beta,\lambda}=\lambda^{-(\beta-2)/(4-\beta)}$. For $\beta\geq4$, we set $R_{\beta,\lambda}=+\infty$, as this estimate gives no algebraic crossover scale in $\lambda$.

